Issue: The parent mixes two readings of a question as raw cosine scores, so the weights it sets are not the weights the readings get, and it never reads the text a case was answered with. `rank_examples` scores a case as `TOKEN_WEIGHT * words + (1 - TOKEN_WEIGHT) * characters` with the two cosines on whatever scales the texts happen to produce, and the scales differ by orders of magnitude: over the frozen memories and the Val questions (no model calls, the query the tool is handed), the best word reading of a LawBench question is 0.0002 against 0.0585 for the character reading, so the half the weights give the word reading is about 0.3% of the order it decides, while for USPTO the same weights give it 36% -- the balance of every comparison is set by the readings' magnitudes rather than by the harness. What a case was answered with is not read at all: the head of the block carries the cases whose facts resemble the question, and the answer the question is asking for -- the charge named after the conduct, the reactants written in the notation of the molecule they are made from -- is only as near as the facts of its case happen to be. The parent's own 50 LawBench questions price what the head decides: a question whose gold entry is carried inside the head is answered 0.82 correctly, one whose gold sits outside it 0.50-0.62, and 22 of the 50 have every gold entry inside the head, carrying 0.573 of the gold's entries between them. The parent's case at [[LawBench/conversations/0031/sample-0.json]] of `runs/bf9c69efaa50709642cd760ec49d71e2743b8c5b/` shows the cost: it writes `盗窃;破坏交通工具` against a gold that carries `以危险方法危害公共安全`, whose memory case the ranking puts at rank 41 of 200.

Modification: the readings are brought to one scale before they are mixed, and the answer a case was answered with is read too. `solver/tools/retrieval.py`: `_scaled` divides each reading's scores by that reading's own best score for this question, so each reading orders the memory from nothing to its best match and the weights are the share of the order each reading decides; `_answer_ngrams` reads the case's answer by the same memory-weight table as everything else, so the punctuation between entries and the notation's own characters decide nothing; `rank_examples` mixes three readings -- `WORD_WEIGHT` 0.4, `CHARACTER_WEIGHT` 0.4 and `ANSWER_WEIGHT` 0.2 -- the words and characters of the case's statement and the characters of its answer. No model call is added, the memory is unchanged, and no dataset name, label list or keyword is used: the readings and their weights are read from the texts the memory writes.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.1833, "Symptom2Disease": 0.87, "LawBench": 0.59, "average": 0.5478, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The mechanism moved the evidence exactly as measured and the average fell 0.0278 from the parent's 0.5756, which one run cannot attribute to it. Offline over the Val questions the delivered head gains what it was built to gain: LawBench's first twenty cases carry every gold entry of 22 -> 31 of the 50 questions (0.573 -> 0.730 of the gold's entries), Symptom2Disease's gold label sits in the head for 46 -> 48 of 50, USPTO's first five carry 4.33 -> 4.40 of the question's own reaction type. LawBench is the task it was aimed at and the one it moved: 0.57 -> 0.59 (tying the lineage's best LawBench), on 12 changed answers, with both samples of [[LawBench/conversations/0031/sample-0.json]] writing the gold `以危险方法危害公共安全` that the new ranking moves from rank 41 to rank 4, and [[LawBench/conversations/0044/sample-0.json]] broken the same way in reverse, the case carrying `强制猥亵、侮辱妇女` moving 24 -> 1 and the answer following it off the gold's `侮辱`. The other two tasks moved the other way on evidence that only changed order: Symptom2Disease's whole 200-case memory is delivered in both runs and its head changed in 98/100 samples, yet 3 answers changed and 2 broke (89 -> 87); USPTO's whole 50-case memory is delivered in both runs, its head changed in 60/60 samples, and 45 of its 60 answers were rewritten for 3 fixed against 8 broken (0.2667 -> 0.1833), the breaks being what the parent's note already priced at +-5 samples of provider churn on an identical block -- [[USPTO/conversations/0028/sample-1.json]] rewrites `NCc1cccnc1` as `c1cccnc1CN`, the same molecule, and [[USPTO/conversations/0023/sample-0.json]] writes `...ccc1CH` for `...ccc1C`. Cost is unchanged: model calls 3.04 / 3.00 / 3.78 per sample, mean elapsed 47.1 / 35.9 / 134.3 s against the parent's 49.4 / 44.0 / 132.9, memory context 61756 / 35218 / 18729 characters, no sample failed